- Story & Context: Confined to their Chelsea cellar during the dense fog of November 1957, June Galloway spent seventy-two continuous hours tracking a dying rubber tree’s bio-electric decline across a glowing oscilloscope screen. She missed her sister’s wedding and let telegrams from the Royal Society rot unopened under the drafty mail slot, unable to tear herself away from the way the plant's fading current made her Harmon-muted trumpet bend into ghostly, microtonal blue notes. When Pendleton finally kicked the front door open, bringing in the smell of cold river coal and winter rain, June was still playing the same suspended minor chord, her lips bloody against the brass mouthpiece. "Why ask for Sunday morning when the xylem keeps the dawn?" she whispered into the vapor, refusing to let the dying epiphyte leave the amplifier circuit.
